David Damerell wrote:
> Scrolls, potions, rings, wands - yes, you want them all, and you want to
> BUC them all.
>
You don't have to be that diligent about BUCing wands, do you?
The only penalty for using a cursed wand is that it might explode,
and I've never had a cursed wand explode on me anyway.
(Checking the spoilers only 3% of wands are generated cursed,
and cursed wands only explode 1% of the time.)

What I'd say is that it's not important to pet-test wands, since
unlike armor and weapons you want them even if they're cursed
(and if you've pet-tested armor you can instantly wear-test its
enchantment, and even quickly ID a lot of the good magical
armor). In fact, it's not that important to pet-test scrolls and
potions unless they're of a kind you're planning to use soon.
Rings and amulets you should absolutely pet-test, and
whenever you get to an altar you should drop-test everything.
(Except possibly scrolls of scare monster, since they degrade
when you drop them.) The important thing is not to put on any
cursed items.

Oh, and don't put on any conical hats or funny helmets even if
they're uncursed, because they can be dunce caps or helms
of opposite alignment, which curse themselves when you put
them on. Actually I believe that only wizards have any reason
to mess around with conical hats anyway, and the helm of
opposite alignment does have its uses, but not only will it
stick to your head, it'll cost you any protection you've bought.

I don't think this necessarily contradicts what David said; just
that there are lots of things you don't need to pet-test right
away, you can wait until you get to the altar to BUC test them.
And once you're there you may as well BUC test everything.

